## Application Server vs Web Server

| | Web Server (Nginx) | Application Server |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary role** | Handles HTTP requests and responses | Executes application business logic |
| **What it serves** | Static files: HTML, CSS, JS, images | Dynamic content: generated per request |
| **Processing** | I/O-bound: many connections, file reads | CPU/memory-bound: code execution, data processing |
| **Protocol** | HTTP/HTTPS directly with clients | Internal protocol with web server (FastCGI, uWSGI, HTTP) |
| **Scalability** | Handles thousands of simultaneous connections | Scales by adding more processes/workers |
| **Examples** | Nginx, Apache | Gunicorn, uWSGI, PHP-FPM, Node.js, Puma |
| **Restart impact** | Restarting drops all connections briefly | Restarting only affects dynamic processing |

**The web server is the front door. The application server is the engine room.**

In a combined setup (Task 0), both run on the same machine and compete for the same resources. In a split setup (Task 3), each operates at peak efficiency within its own dedicated environment.
